WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.240
So imagine you have this highly sensitive digital file.

00:03.440 --> 00:08.960
Like, let's say it's a document with literally all your passwords, your bank routing numbers, I mean, just everything.

00:09.360 --> 00:11.320
Right. Your entire life just sitting in a file.

00:11.520 --> 00:13.800
Exactly. And obviously, you want to keep it safe.

00:14.080 --> 00:17.020
So you lock it inside this high-security digital vault.

00:17.240 --> 00:17.640
Makes sense.

00:18.040 --> 00:19.320
But you don't stop there, right?

00:19.640 --> 00:22.240
You take that vault and you put it inside another vault.

00:22.600 --> 00:28.540
Then you, like, bury that second vault ten folders deep inside some hidden directory on a really secure server.

00:28.540 --> 00:30.540
And you probably feel incredibly safe doing that.

00:30.720 --> 00:38.180
Oh, absolutely. I mean, visually, mathematically, you feel like you have created this impenetrable fortress of nested layers.

00:38.960 --> 00:50.820
But what if I told you that in the bizarre, totally hidden architecture of computer science, putting that file deeper into the vault might have just accidentally granted it the power to delete your entire hard drive?

00:51.360 --> 00:57.680
It is a genuinely terrifying thought. But mathematically speaking, it is entirely possible.

00:57.680 --> 01:00.600
Wait, really? Just by putting it deeper in a folder?

01:01.200 --> 01:08.120
Yes. Because, well, we rely so heavily on our physical intuition when we interact with computers.

01:08.680 --> 01:14.560
But digital geometry, it absolutely does not obey the laws of physical space.

01:14.800 --> 01:18.740
And that profound disconnect is exactly what we are exploring today.

01:18.980 --> 01:25.240
You know, usually when we think about physical architecture, there is this expectation of just, well, absolute rigidity.

01:25.240 --> 01:26.360
Right, like a building.

01:26.600 --> 01:33.020
Yeah. You look at a blueprint, the walls are these thick black lines, and the architect just points and says, you know, there is the room.

01:33.280 --> 01:36.900
That is the boundary. You are either inside the room or you are outside the room.

01:37.100 --> 01:39.620
It's binary. It's clean. It's comforting.

01:40.260 --> 01:45.280
I mean, human beings just really like things to be visible and categorized into neat little containers.

01:45.280 --> 01:50.960
Exactly. And we carry that exact same physical expectation right into our digital lives.

01:51.360 --> 01:57.920
When you, the listener, double-click a folder on your computer, your brain naturally tells you that you are now inside that folder.

01:58.120 --> 01:59.720
You feel physically located in it.

01:59.800 --> 02:05.220
Right. When you run a Python script, you expect the code to execute inside its little block.

02:05.480 --> 02:10.860
When your character walks through a dungeon door in a video game, you absolutely expect to be inside the next room.

02:10.860 --> 02:13.960
But what if inside is just a hallucination?

02:14.160 --> 02:22.760
Oh, it is a hallucination. It's a very useful one, obviously designed by programmers to keep us from totally losing our minds, but a hallucination nonetheless.

02:23.160 --> 02:24.580
So it's just a UI trick.

02:24.880 --> 02:32.880
Basically, yeah. Because when you step into the actual mathematical world of computational contexts, that beautiful rigid blueprint completely shatters.

02:33.300 --> 02:37.840
We are actually looking at an architectural landscape that is, well, it's highly deceptive.

02:37.840 --> 02:41.160
It is the absolute definition of an architectural illusion.

02:41.820 --> 02:44.700
And that is exactly our mission for today's deep dive.

02:45.040 --> 02:52.720
We are taking a giant magnifying glass to a brilliant, independent, 2026 paper called The Geometry of Scope.

02:53.120 --> 02:59.740
Yep. Subtitled Context, Visibility, and Translation Across Formal Systems by a researcher named Flyxion.

03:00.220 --> 03:04.760
Right. And our goal today is to really dissect this idea of context.

03:04.760 --> 03:12.940
Every single time you open a folder or write a line of code or explore a virtual world, you are interacting with context.

03:13.260 --> 03:13.840
And it's everywhere.

03:14.340 --> 03:21.760
It is. So we are going to explore the hidden mathematical framework that links all these wildly diverse digital worlds together.

03:21.760 --> 03:34.320
And by the end of this hour, I promise we are going to prove to you that these spaces are entirely different, totally bizarre geometric shapes that are just masquerading as simple, familiar trees.

03:34.860 --> 03:36.680
Masquerading is really the perfect word for it.

03:37.020 --> 03:44.720
I mean, we intuitively think of our digital environments like folders, file systems, game levels as these nested branching trees.

03:44.940 --> 03:45.980
Right. Like a family tree.

03:45.980 --> 03:50.740
Exactly. A root folder, branching into subfolders, branching into individual files.

03:51.320 --> 03:56.640
But to truly understand the math of context, we first have to completely abandon that assumption.

03:57.020 --> 04:01.300
Okay. Let's unpack this because I need to push back on this fundamental premise right away.

04:01.720 --> 04:04.320
Why is it so wrong to just think of my files as a tree?

04:04.660 --> 04:05.580
Well, because...

04:05.580 --> 04:11.860
Like I turn on my computer, I open my C drive, I click into my documents folder, and I open my taxes folder.

04:11.960 --> 04:15.460
That is a straight line down a branch. It's a tree. What is the problem with that model?

04:15.460 --> 04:20.400
Well, it's not a problem if your system is exclusively a classic nested hierarchy.

04:20.800 --> 04:24.520
In a pure hierarchy, you have what mathematicians actually call a rooted tree.

04:25.380 --> 04:26.420
Okay. Rooted tree.

04:26.540 --> 04:31.940
Yeah. Meaning every single context, every single folder has exactly one immediate container.

04:32.500 --> 04:36.000
Your taxes folder has exactly one parent, which is the documents folder.

04:36.440 --> 04:38.860
The documents folder has one parent, the C drive.

04:39.440 --> 04:41.080
Right. That works perfectly.

04:41.540 --> 04:42.640
It works. Yes.

04:42.640 --> 04:46.440
But let me ask you, have you ever created a shortcut on your desktop?

04:47.240 --> 04:50.060
Oh, of course. Literally all the time my desktop is a mess.

04:50.280 --> 04:52.340
Or maybe a hard link in a Unix system.

04:52.760 --> 04:59.940
The thing is, when you do that, you suddenly have a file or a directory that effectively exists in multiple places at the exact same time.

05:00.280 --> 05:02.000
Oh, I see where you're going with this.

05:02.000 --> 05:07.760
Right. It can be accessed from the desktop, and it can also be accessed from deep within the system drive.

05:08.360 --> 05:13.720
So the moment you introduce that hard link, that file suddenly has two incomparable parents.

05:14.100 --> 05:17.460
Wow. Okay, yeah, that completely breaks the tree shape, doesn't it?

05:17.720 --> 05:18.140
Completely.

05:18.140 --> 05:24.580
Mathematically, a structure where a single node has more than one parent simply cannot be a tree.

05:25.500 --> 05:29.920
The rigid geometry breaks, and it becomes what we call a directed graph.

05:30.520 --> 05:31.000
Oh, I see.

05:31.460 --> 05:33.420
So it's a graph with multiple parents.

05:33.980 --> 05:35.840
And it's not just files, either.

05:36.480 --> 05:39.600
If I think about programming, what about like a shared module?

05:39.880 --> 05:40.940
That's a perfect example.

05:40.940 --> 05:49.820
Like if I write a piece of code that calculates dates, and three completely different software applications on my server all pull from that exact same date module,

05:50.000 --> 05:51.940
that module doesn't just live on one branch.

05:52.000 --> 05:54.240
It's plugged into all three programs simultaneously.

05:54.520 --> 05:54.980
Precisely.

05:55.440 --> 06:01.860
And if we try to map that as a tree, we are just forcing a complex web into a shape that absolutely doesn't fit.

06:02.440 --> 06:06.700
But, you know, it gets even weirder when you introduce graphs with cycles.

06:07.560 --> 06:08.000
Cycles.

06:08.340 --> 06:09.220
Like loops.

06:09.480 --> 06:10.860
Yes, exactly, loops.

06:10.940 --> 06:14.600
Think about mutually recursive modules in software architecture.

06:15.440 --> 06:18.600
Module A absolutely cannot function without calling module B.

06:19.400 --> 06:22.260
But module B cannot function without calling module A.

06:22.700 --> 06:23.180
Oh, man.

06:23.260 --> 06:24.660
They just point right back at each other.

06:24.880 --> 06:25.220
They do.

06:25.800 --> 06:31.580
Or, you know, to use a more spatial example that might be easier to visualize, think about portals in a video game.

06:31.880 --> 06:33.340
Oh, like the actual game Portal.

06:33.780 --> 06:35.900
Or really any game with non-Euclidean geometry.

06:36.240 --> 06:36.460
Right.

06:36.460 --> 06:41.420
So you walk through a door on the left side of room A and you instantly end up in room B.

06:41.920 --> 06:46.300
You walk through a door in room B and you are instantly deposited right back into room A.

06:46.300 --> 06:50.800
Okay, if I tried to draw that as a tree diagram, I would literally lose my mind.

06:50.800 --> 06:57.180
I draw room A, then I draw a line down to room B, then I draw a line down to room A again, then room B.

06:57.460 --> 06:59.920
You'd just be drawing an infinite vine down the page.

06:59.920 --> 07:01.600
Yeah, it would literally never stop.

07:01.820 --> 07:06.460
Which is exactly why the tree model is a complete mathematical failure for these types of systems.

07:07.440 --> 07:11.920
In graph theory, when we map out everywhere you can possibly reach from a given starting point...

07:12.640 --> 07:14.640
Which is what, the whole universe of options?

07:15.080 --> 07:15.940
Basically, yeah.

07:16.240 --> 07:19.140
The formal term is the reflexive transitive closure.

07:19.800 --> 07:25.760
When you map that out, a cycle collapses all of those interconnected rooms into one single equivalence class.

07:26.120 --> 07:29.840
Meaning they're basically treated as the same mathematical blob.

07:30.160 --> 07:30.540
Exactly.

07:31.220 --> 07:37.120
A basic tree structure just cannot distinguish a cycle of portals from a simple list of aliases.

07:37.920 --> 07:39.700
The geometry completely breaks down.

07:40.160 --> 07:43.300
So, the tree illusion is dead.

07:43.920 --> 07:46.820
We are living in a world of complex, tangled graphs.

07:46.820 --> 07:52.840
But if the structure is this tangled, how does the computer actually know where I am at any given moment?

07:53.220 --> 07:54.580
That is the million-dollar question.

07:54.940 --> 07:58.500
Because Flyxion talks about this in Proposition 2.3, right?

07:58.500 --> 08:03.120
Detailing this really sharp distinction between a route and an endpoint.

08:03.500 --> 08:06.320
What is the computer actually calculating in the background?

08:06.820 --> 08:11.480
So, this is a massive divide in how digital systems understand location.

08:12.440 --> 08:14.900
Let's say our system is that tangled directed graph.

08:15.640 --> 08:19.600
The computer can actually record your location in one of two very distinct ways.

08:19.600 --> 08:20.340
Okay.

08:20.800 --> 08:21.620
What are they?

08:22.100 --> 08:24.760
Well, it can record the actual path you took to get there.

08:25.100 --> 08:27.540
Like the sequence of links you clicked, which is the route.

08:28.620 --> 08:31.460
Or, it can just record the reachability.

08:31.960 --> 08:34.280
The final node you landed on, which is the endpoint.

08:34.660 --> 08:35.600
Just the destination.

08:35.600 --> 08:36.320
Exactly.

08:36.760 --> 08:40.120
And the mathematical theories for these two approaches are fundamentally different.

08:40.860 --> 08:50.940
Flyxion states that a functor from the path category to the reachability preorder is only faithful if the graph has at most one directed path between any two vertices.

08:51.400 --> 08:52.140
Whoa, whoa, hold on.

08:52.620 --> 08:56.720
A functor, reachability preorder, you completely lost me there.

08:57.300 --> 08:58.800
Let's bring this back to Earth.

08:59.100 --> 09:00.120
Let me try an analogy.

09:00.500 --> 09:01.220
Okay, go for it.

09:01.220 --> 09:12.540
Is this like the difference between giving someone turn-by-turn directions to a restaurant, like take a left on Main, a right on Oak, and go through the alley, versus just texting them the exact GPS coordinates of the restaurant?

09:13.140 --> 09:14.780
That's actually a really good start, yeah.

09:15.020 --> 09:20.380
But let's push the analogy a bit further to understand why the computer actually cares which one you use.

09:20.560 --> 09:20.920
Okay.

09:21.660 --> 09:24.760
Let's say you take the turn-by-turn route through the alley, right?

09:25.000 --> 09:26.740
And your friend takes the main highway.

09:26.740 --> 09:32.080
But you both end up at the exact same GPS coordinates, sitting at a table in the restaurant.

09:32.640 --> 09:34.120
Right, we're at the exact same endpoint.

09:34.480 --> 09:34.860
Exactly.

09:35.360 --> 09:43.120
Now, if the system only tracks the endpoint, the GPS coordinates, it completely discards the entire history of how you got there.

09:43.500 --> 09:45.680
It just knows user is at table 5.

09:46.220 --> 09:49.500
Right, which seems totally fine if all we care about is eating dinner.

09:49.740 --> 09:50.920
Why does it matter how I got there?

09:50.920 --> 09:57.440
But what if taking the highway required paying a toll, and taking the alley required knowing a secret password?

09:58.340 --> 09:59.140
Oh, I see.

09:59.580 --> 09:59.800
Right.

10:00.020 --> 10:06.620
If the computer wants to let you retrace your steps or reverse your actions, the endpoint model fails catastrophically.

10:07.140 --> 10:11.240
It literally doesn't know if it should ask you for the toll or the password on your way out.

10:11.240 --> 10:19.500
But, if it tracks the route, the exact arrows you traversed in that graph, it retains the entire history of your journey.

10:20.180 --> 10:21.660
Okay, I totally see that now.

10:22.200 --> 10:30.340
So, if there is only one single road to the restaurant, the GPS coordinate and the turn-by-turn directions effectively contain the exact same amount of useful information.

10:30.760 --> 10:31.100
Yes.

10:31.100 --> 10:37.540
And that is exactly what I meant by the math only being faithful if there is, at most, one directed path.

10:38.000 --> 10:38.360
Right.

10:39.080 --> 10:47.780
But the moment you have two ways to get to the same file, like a shortcut on your desktop and the actual file buried deep in your C drive, the endpoint model loses vital information.

10:48.360 --> 10:48.780
Exactly.

10:48.780 --> 10:58.400
A system that tracks the route will treat arriving via the desktop shortcut as a completely different mathematical state than arriving via the C drive deep dive.

10:58.400 --> 11:02.220
Even though you are looking at the exact same document.

11:02.660 --> 11:03.400
That makes total sense.

11:03.560 --> 11:09.960
And we're going to see how that route versus endpoint confusion causes actual chaos in file systems a bit later.

11:10.260 --> 11:16.340
But before we get there, we really need to talk about what you can actually do once you've arrived at your destination.

11:16.600 --> 11:16.780
Right.

11:16.880 --> 11:17.360
The permissions.

11:18.000 --> 11:18.220
Yeah.

11:18.660 --> 11:21.040
So, we know where we are in a context structure.

11:21.400 --> 11:24.240
The natural next question is, what can we do there?

11:24.240 --> 11:28.400
And this brings us to a concept the paper calls the independence principle.

11:28.800 --> 11:32.740
I have to say, this part totally upended how I think about digital permissions.

11:33.380 --> 11:38.440
It is incredibly counterintuitive to how human beings normally organize space.

11:39.560 --> 11:45.300
Phlyoxian introduces these three independent subsets that exist at literally any given context.

11:45.660 --> 11:45.880
Okay.

11:46.040 --> 11:46.860
Lay them out for us.

11:47.160 --> 11:49.960
So, first we have K, which represents knowledge.

11:50.540 --> 11:53.720
This is all the information available at that specific location.

11:53.720 --> 11:54.760
K is knowledge.

11:54.980 --> 11:55.320
Got it.

11:55.860 --> 12:01.080
And we have V, which is visibility, or basically the presentation of that knowledge to the user.

12:01.680 --> 12:06.720
And finally, we have A, which is authority, meaning the actions you are actually permitted to execute.

12:07.200 --> 12:07.440
Okay.

12:07.560 --> 12:10.400
So, K is knowledge, V is visibility, A is authority.

12:10.760 --> 12:11.300
Simple enough.

12:11.500 --> 12:20.320
And the really critical theorem here, Proposition 3.1, states there is no forced monotonicity between these subsets and the depth of the hierarchy.

12:20.960 --> 12:21.200
Okay.

12:21.240 --> 12:22.140
Let's unpack this.

12:22.140 --> 12:24.840
What is no forced monotonicity?

12:25.180 --> 12:26.040
Break that down for us.

12:26.100 --> 12:28.340
What exactly is monotonicity in this context?

12:29.060 --> 12:36.040
Well, monotonicity in mathematics basically describes a function that moves consistently in one single direction.

12:36.380 --> 12:39.920
It either only ever increases or it only ever decreases.

12:40.280 --> 12:41.880
So, it never zigzags.

12:41.880 --> 12:42.540
Exactly.

12:42.700 --> 12:43.440
No zigzagging.

12:43.720 --> 12:45.100
Think about physical space.

12:45.420 --> 12:55.780
If you put a small box inside a medium box and put that inside a large box, the physical capacity of those boxes decreases monotonically.

12:56.000 --> 12:58.060
The deeper you go, the smaller the volume.

12:58.260 --> 13:00.780
It is a strict rule of physics.

13:00.780 --> 13:01.700
Right.

13:01.700 --> 13:03.140
It's like Russian nesting dolls.

13:03.260 --> 13:07.600
The innermost doll is always, strictly, undeniably the smallest.

13:07.800 --> 13:09.000
It has to be or it wouldn't fit.

13:09.440 --> 13:09.760
Exactly.

13:10.560 --> 13:18.340
And because we use these physical metaphors like folders for our digital lives, we naturally assume digital hierarchies work the exact same way.

13:18.480 --> 13:19.060
We totally do.

13:19.060 --> 13:28.360
We just assume that as you go deeper into a nested structure, your visibility must shrink sequentially or your authority must be reduced sequentially.

13:29.040 --> 13:31.560
But Phyachesis proves that is a complete myth.

13:32.200 --> 13:33.620
A very dangerous myth, honestly.

13:34.000 --> 13:43.460
Hierarchical containment, just being nested inside something else, enforces zero mathematical rules about how knowledge, visibility, or authority behave.

13:44.000 --> 13:45.380
They are completely independent.

13:46.160 --> 13:47.940
Completely independent assignments.

13:47.940 --> 13:50.820
Okay, let's make this really concrete for the listener.

13:51.360 --> 13:53.040
Think about a hidden file on your computer.

13:53.760 --> 13:56.000
That file exists in K, right?

13:56.080 --> 13:57.880
The total knowledge available in that folder.

13:58.300 --> 14:01.020
The hard drive physically knows the file is sitting right there.

14:01.180 --> 14:01.380
Right.

14:01.460 --> 14:02.620
The system knows it's there.

14:03.020 --> 14:11.080
But unless you have specifically gone into your settings and clicked, you know, show hidden files, it does not exist in V, the visibility.

14:11.880 --> 14:12.320
Exactly.

14:12.820 --> 14:14.740
Visibility is just a subset of knowledge.

14:14.740 --> 14:21.820
The graphical interface can't show you a file that doesn't exist, but it can absolutely hide things that do exist.

14:21.820 --> 14:24.300
And then imagine a read-only file.

14:24.300 --> 14:26.000
It is in V, right?

14:26.080 --> 14:28.480
You can clearly see the icon sitting right there on your desktop.

14:28.860 --> 14:33.300
But if you try to hit delete or try to edit it, the computer throws an error.

14:33.960 --> 14:37.200
The delete action is just not in A, your authority.

14:37.200 --> 14:38.200
Exactly.

14:38.200 --> 14:44.860
And to prove just how wild this gets, Valetian provides this brilliant finite counterexample in the paper.

14:45.400 --> 14:47.680
They construct a basic three-element chain.

14:48.260 --> 14:49.100
Okay, walk us through it.

14:49.700 --> 14:57.100
So context one is the outer layer, context two is nested inside one, and context three is nested inside two.

14:57.860 --> 15:01.460
It is a strict hierarchy, exactly like your Russian nesting dolls.

15:01.460 --> 15:05.960
Okay, I'm picturing three nesting dolls. Outer, middle, inner.

15:06.500 --> 15:09.880
Now, let's look at how knowledge is assigned in their mathematical proof.

15:10.500 --> 15:14.440
The total universe of information items are just the numbers one, two, three, and four.

15:14.860 --> 15:16.120
Keep it simple. I like it.

15:16.360 --> 15:20.600
So at the outermost layer, context one, the knowledge is the set one, two.

15:21.100 --> 15:26.560
You open the doll, step deeper into context two, and the knowledge shrinks to just the set one.

15:26.560 --> 15:31.560
Okay, that makes perfect sense so far. We went deeper, the box got smaller, we see less.

15:31.700 --> 15:33.740
It feels like a very secure sandbox.

15:34.340 --> 15:39.680
But then, you open the middle doll and step deeper again, into the innermost layer, context three.

15:40.280 --> 15:44.440
And suddenly, out of nowhere, the knowledge set expands to one, three.

15:45.220 --> 15:45.760
Wait, what?

15:46.280 --> 15:54.520
It shrunk from one, two down to just one, but then it bounced back up and grabbed a completely new piece of data, the three?

15:54.520 --> 16:01.260
Yes. The available information went from one, two, to one, and then suddenly to one, three.

16:01.920 --> 16:06.440
That completely breaks the idea of a smooth, predictable, monotonic reduction.

16:06.980 --> 16:08.260
It totally zigzagged.

16:08.500 --> 16:12.940
It did. It is neither consistently increasing nor consistently decreasing.

16:13.300 --> 16:15.800
That completely ruins the nesting doll metaphor.

16:16.140 --> 16:20.960
The innermost doll somehow holds an object that the outer dolls didn't even know existed.

16:20.960 --> 16:25.440
And it gets so much worse when you apply this erratic behavior to authority.

16:26.320 --> 16:31.180
In Flyxion's example, the authority at the outermost layer is just the right to read.

16:31.560 --> 16:35.820
At the middle layer, it changes to just the right to write.

16:36.380 --> 16:36.700
Okay.

16:36.880 --> 16:42.060
And at the deepest, innermost layer, you suddenly have the right to read, write, and execute.

16:42.440 --> 16:43.080
Oh my God.

16:43.540 --> 16:46.020
The security implications of this are just staggering.

16:46.020 --> 16:50.060
Going back to my hook at the beginning of the show, you know, the vault inside the vault.

16:50.460 --> 16:50.540
Right.

16:51.000 --> 17:01.360
It is this massive human bias to assume that if you put a program into a sandbox, like a nested subfolder, it strictly has less power than the parent folder.

17:01.660 --> 17:02.860
Yes, everyone assumes that.

17:03.020 --> 17:07.220
But mathematically, nesting alone enforces zero rules about authority.

17:07.220 --> 17:15.740
You could step into a deeper, buried subfolder, execute a file, and suddenly trigger a script that grants you root access to the entire machine.

17:16.220 --> 17:20.740
What's fascinating here is that that is precisely the danger Flyxion is highlighting.

17:21.140 --> 17:27.180
If you rely on the geometry of the tree to enforce your security, you are relying on an illusion.

17:27.800 --> 17:30.760
It's just a feeling of safety, not actual math.

17:30.760 --> 17:31.240
Exactly.

17:31.780 --> 17:37.820
The Unix operating system actually has a feature called a set UID program that behaves exactly like this.

17:38.520 --> 17:45.840
You execute a file that is buried deep down in the hierarchy, but upon entry, your authority suddenly spikes to match the file's owner.

17:46.260 --> 17:47.700
Who might be the sysadmin.

17:48.000 --> 17:48.520
Exactly.

17:48.520 --> 17:53.100
Your power violently expands, completely ignoring the depth of the hierarchy.

17:54.120 --> 18:00.240
The geometry of scope proves that treating an execution environment like a physical container is a massive vulnerability.

18:00.760 --> 18:05.340
Okay, so knowledge, visibility, and authority are totally independent of depth.

18:05.520 --> 18:08.000
The nesting dolls are lying to us.

18:08.440 --> 18:15.620
But if these attributes are separate from where you physically are in the system, how do those things change when you actually move around?

18:15.620 --> 18:18.340
I mean, I'm navigating through folders on my computer all day long.

18:19.220 --> 18:22.080
That brings us to a really fascinating mathematical puzzle.

18:22.620 --> 18:25.540
The difference between moving and squinting.

18:25.780 --> 18:26.620
Moving and squinting.

18:26.620 --> 18:32.740
Or as Flyxion informally defines it in Proposition 4.1, the commutation criteria.

18:33.300 --> 18:45.900
This explores the strict mathematical separation between navigating, which is changing your context, moving from folder to folder, and disclosing, which is changing your visibility settings within a context.

18:45.900 --> 18:55.280
So navigating is double-clicking a new folder, disclosing is toggling a setting like hitting a keyboard shortcut to show hidden files, or show file extensions?

18:55.780 --> 18:56.100
Correct.

18:56.600 --> 19:00.220
And the big mathematical question is, do these two operations commute?

19:00.880 --> 19:01.320
Commute?

19:01.860 --> 19:02.080
Yeah.

19:02.220 --> 19:07.580
In basic algebra, operations commute if changing their order doesn't change the final result.

19:07.680 --> 19:10.620
Like 3 plus 5 is 8, 5 plus 3 is also 8.

19:11.080 --> 19:12.080
The order doesn't matter.

19:12.180 --> 19:12.420
Right.

19:12.520 --> 19:13.220
Addition commutes.

19:13.220 --> 19:22.700
But in a digital system, does navigating and then disclosing yield the exact same mathematical state as disclosing and then navigating?

19:23.220 --> 19:23.380
Okay.

19:23.560 --> 19:26.400
Let me try another analogy here to wrap my head around this.

19:27.100 --> 19:29.300
Think about wearing a pair of polarized sunglasses.

19:30.120 --> 19:30.440
Okay.

19:30.640 --> 19:31.020
Sunglasses.

19:31.680 --> 19:33.840
Those sunglasses are your disclosure setting, right?

19:33.940 --> 19:38.300
They change your visibility, cutting out glare so you can see hidden things in the water.

19:38.300 --> 19:47.780
Now, do you put the sunglasses on before you walk from the beach into a glaring room, or do you put them on after you cross the threshold into the room?

19:48.280 --> 19:54.900
That's close, but it's actually a bit more dangerous than just sunglasses, because sunglasses only affect you, the observer.

19:55.300 --> 19:55.600
True.

19:55.600 --> 20:00.100
But what if the act of disclosing actually interacts with the rules of the room itself?

20:01.040 --> 20:07.760
The question Proposition 4.1 asks is, does crossing the threshold mathematically force the sunglasses off your face?

20:08.360 --> 20:11.900
Or does the room refuse to let you in if you're already wearing them?

20:12.900 --> 20:16.880
So if I put them on, then walk in, am I still wearing them?

20:17.220 --> 20:19.660
Does the system actually respect my choice?

20:19.660 --> 20:23.200
It all depends on what Flyxion calls the intertwining condition.

20:24.240 --> 20:29.440
It's really about how the system's policy carries your display settings across a geometric transition.

20:30.340 --> 20:33.740
The paper breaks this down by looking at three real-world file manager behaviors.

20:34.300 --> 20:35.540
Let's actually walk through them.

20:35.720 --> 20:37.720
Let's do it. Walk me through the first scenario.

20:38.280 --> 20:40.380
So, scenario one is the global toggle.

20:41.300 --> 20:44.960
In this system, your sunglasses are effectively super-glued to your face.

20:45.640 --> 20:47.160
Okay. I like that.

20:47.160 --> 20:50.520
The show hidden file setting is a global variable.

20:51.340 --> 20:55.500
If you turn it on in folder A and then navigate to folder B, it stays on.

20:56.220 --> 21:00.100
In this scenario, navigating and disclosing absolutely do commute.

21:00.400 --> 21:02.220
Because the order doesn't change anything.

21:02.420 --> 21:02.820
Exactly.

21:03.240 --> 21:06.140
Because whether you toggle the setting and then move to folder B,

21:06.540 --> 21:08.860
or you move to folder B and then toggle the setting,

21:09.280 --> 21:11.480
the final mathematical state is identical.

21:12.080 --> 21:15.000
You're standing in folder B with hidden files visible.

21:15.420 --> 21:16.940
Okay. That's the easy one.

21:17.160 --> 21:17.520
Right.

21:17.700 --> 21:19.520
Three plus five equals five plus three.

21:19.860 --> 21:20.600
Order doesn't matter.

21:21.000 --> 21:21.280
Right.

21:22.220 --> 21:24.860
But scenario two is the per-folder toggle.

21:25.840 --> 21:29.580
Here, the system remembers a specific preference for each individual folder.

21:29.580 --> 21:31.640
You are standing in folder A.

21:32.220 --> 21:34.140
You toggle show hidden files on.

21:34.620 --> 21:35.980
Then you navigate to folder B.

21:36.700 --> 21:37.500
What happens?

21:38.460 --> 21:38.900
Oh, wait.

21:39.040 --> 21:40.200
Folder B has its own memory.

21:40.720 --> 21:45.140
So, if folder B was previously set by me to hide files,

21:45.280 --> 21:47.600
I cross the threshold and the files instantly disappear.

21:48.140 --> 21:52.260
It's like the room has its own lighting rules that completely override my sunglasses.

21:52.260 --> 21:52.900
Exactly.

21:52.900 --> 21:55.820
In this case, the operations do not commute.

21:56.540 --> 22:00.400
If you toggle the setting in folder A and then move to folder B,

22:00.700 --> 22:04.780
you arrive and you are subjected to folder B's default visibility.

22:05.260 --> 22:05.640
Right.

22:05.640 --> 22:09.180
But if you move to folder B first and then hit the toggle,

22:09.520 --> 22:12.240
you are actively changing folder B's setting to show.

22:12.960 --> 22:16.820
The final state of your visibility is completely different depending on the sequence of operations.

22:17.520 --> 22:20.100
Move, then toggle, yields visibility.

22:20.740 --> 22:22.780
Toggle, then move, yields blindness.

22:23.460 --> 22:23.680
Wow.

22:23.800 --> 22:23.980
Okay.

22:24.060 --> 22:25.280
And it gets even more strict, right?

22:25.360 --> 22:27.120
The paper mentions a third scenario?

22:27.480 --> 22:27.760
Yes.

22:27.960 --> 22:28.740
The default reset.

22:29.640 --> 22:33.720
This is a highly restrictive system where entering literally any new context

22:33.720 --> 22:38.080
automatically wipes your visibility settings right back to a strict factory default.

22:38.540 --> 22:38.900
Ouch.

22:39.300 --> 22:39.500
Yeah.

22:39.780 --> 22:43.040
If you toggle show hidden files on and then you move,

22:43.040 --> 22:46.400
the very act of navigating destroys your disclosure setting.

22:47.180 --> 22:49.280
The threshold rips the sunglasses right off your face.

22:50.140 --> 22:52.620
But if you move first and then toggle the setting,

22:53.120 --> 22:55.780
the disclosure survives because you've already crossed the threshold.

22:56.580 --> 22:58.380
I get the theory, but I have to ask, like,

22:58.460 --> 23:00.900
why does this level of pedantic math matter?

23:01.500 --> 23:04.580
Outside of a theoretical paper, who actually cares if the operations commute?

23:05.100 --> 23:09.360
A programmer who is trying not to destroy a company's data cares immensely.

23:09.520 --> 23:10.240
Okay, fair point.

23:10.540 --> 23:14.680
If a programmer assumes these operations commute when they mathematically don't,

23:15.200 --> 23:18.420
they will introduce incredibly subtle, devastating bugs.

23:18.980 --> 23:21.100
Give me a real-world example of that.

23:21.100 --> 23:23.880
Okay, imagine you write an automated backup script.

23:24.560 --> 23:28.020
The script's job is to navigate into a highly secured directory,

23:28.580 --> 23:31.560
reveal the hidden system files, and back them up to a server.

23:31.840 --> 23:32.460
Simple enough?

23:32.620 --> 23:32.840
Right.

23:32.840 --> 23:36.540
If you write that script for an operating system that uses a global toggle,

23:36.920 --> 23:41.660
you can program it to issue the reveal command first and then navigate into the directory.

23:41.940 --> 23:42.820
It works perfectly.

23:43.100 --> 23:44.520
Because it's super glued to your face.

23:44.720 --> 23:45.120
Exactly.

23:45.700 --> 23:50.880
But a year later, your company ports that script to a new server that uses a per-folder toggle,

23:51.380 --> 23:52.680
or a reset policy.

23:53.080 --> 23:54.380
Oh no, I see where this is going.

23:54.380 --> 23:55.020
Exactly.

23:55.340 --> 24:00.060
The script runs, it issues the reveal command, then it navigates into the secure directory.

24:00.560 --> 24:04.000
But crossing that threshold wipes the visibility setting.

24:04.340 --> 24:05.380
So it's blind again.

24:05.520 --> 24:05.780
Yes.

24:06.000 --> 24:11.940
The script looks around, sees no files, backs up a completely empty folder, and reports success.

24:12.620 --> 24:13.720
It silently fails.

24:14.480 --> 24:18.920
And when the server crashes a month later, you realize your backups are entirely empty.

24:18.920 --> 24:20.320
That is horrifying.

24:20.700 --> 24:25.680
A catastrophic failure, just because the programmer didn't realize that navigating and disclosing

24:25.680 --> 24:27.600
don't commute on the new system.

24:27.820 --> 24:30.880
You really have to know the hidden geometry of the room you're walking into.

24:30.960 --> 24:31.480
You really do.

24:31.900 --> 24:33.680
And that is actually the perfect segue.

24:34.220 --> 24:37.180
Because Flyxion doesn't just leave this as abstract math.

24:37.560 --> 24:42.400
They apply this framework to four incredibly different digital environments that we use every

24:42.400 --> 24:45.740
single day to prove just how diverse these geometries really are.

24:45.900 --> 24:46.160
Yes.

24:46.400 --> 24:47.460
The four case studies.

24:47.460 --> 24:53.200
Flyxion uses these to definitively prove that treating all digital environments as equivalent

24:53.200 --> 24:56.080
trees is a massive mathematical falsehood.

24:56.340 --> 24:58.900
They operate under entirely different laws of physics.

24:59.300 --> 25:00.720
Okay, let's break these down.

25:01.420 --> 25:04.860
Case study one focuses on file systems and shells.

25:05.580 --> 25:07.700
This covers proposition 5.1.

25:08.440 --> 25:12.380
The paper contrasts the permanence and scope of three very common Unix commands.

25:12.380 --> 25:18.560
You've got cd for change directory, launching a subshell, and change mod for changing file

25:18.560 --> 25:19.040
permissions.

25:19.800 --> 25:23.900
Most people who use the command line use these commands basically interchangeably, without

25:23.900 --> 25:25.820
ever thinking about their structural differences.

25:26.480 --> 25:30.840
But Flyxion points out they operate on entirely different mathematical planes.

25:31.400 --> 25:31.740
How so?

25:31.740 --> 25:37.480
Well, when you type cd to change your directory, you are merely changing the geometric position

25:37.480 --> 25:38.820
of your current process.

25:38.820 --> 25:42.220
You are simply moving your observer to a new room.

25:42.220 --> 25:42.620
Right.

25:42.720 --> 25:43.840
I'm just walking down the hall.

25:44.340 --> 25:48.840
But when you launch a subshell, which is a child process spawned from your main terminal,

25:49.180 --> 25:50.100
you aren't just moving.

25:50.640 --> 25:54.060
You are creating a scoped extension of the entire process tree.

25:54.280 --> 25:54.600
Okay.

25:54.600 --> 25:58.000
The child receives a snapshot copy of the parent's universe.

25:58.500 --> 26:00.480
But here is the really critical difference.

26:01.220 --> 26:05.940
Any changes you make to the environment inside that subshell completely vanish the moment you

26:05.940 --> 26:06.520
type exit.

26:06.740 --> 26:07.160
Oh, wow.

26:07.580 --> 26:07.800
Yeah.

26:08.160 --> 26:10.900
The subshell is literally a temporary pocket dimension.

26:11.320 --> 26:14.500
It collapses and leaves the parent universe totally unharmed.

26:14.620 --> 26:14.900
Okay.

26:15.060 --> 26:17.180
So cd is moving within the world.

26:17.660 --> 26:20.600
Subshell is creating a temporary parallel universe.

26:21.200 --> 26:22.580
What about chamod?

26:23.040 --> 26:23.280
Right.

26:23.280 --> 26:27.740
Chamod modifies the shared physical file system itself.

26:28.240 --> 26:31.840
It changes the mathematical authority assignment of a shared component.

26:32.600 --> 26:36.080
Its effects persist forever or until explicitly changed again.

26:36.580 --> 26:41.540
And those effects are seen by every single process on the system, not just your personal pocket

26:41.540 --> 26:41.980
universe.

26:42.740 --> 26:44.340
And here's where it gets really fun.

26:44.560 --> 26:48.700
The paper points out a specific sequence of commands that proves these actions absolutely

26:48.700 --> 26:49.700
do not commute.

26:49.960 --> 26:50.580
They do not.

26:51.000 --> 26:52.520
Let's say you own a directory.

26:52.520 --> 26:59.260
You can type cd to move into that directory and then type chamod 000 to strip all read,

26:59.400 --> 27:00.980
write, and execute permissions from it.

27:01.080 --> 27:01.940
That works, right?

27:02.040 --> 27:06.160
You are standing inside a room and you successfully board up the doors and windows from the inside.

27:06.320 --> 27:07.280
It works perfectly.

27:07.280 --> 27:11.100
But try reversing that mathematical order.

27:11.600 --> 27:11.840
Right.

27:11.840 --> 27:20.500
If you are standing outside the directory and you type change mod 000 first, you just boarded up the doors and windows from the outside.

27:21.020 --> 27:27.160
If you then try to type cd to move into the directory, the computer will explicitly deny you access.

27:27.160 --> 27:28.160
Exactly.

27:28.160 --> 27:33.620
The sequence cd then change mod succeeds, but change mod then cd completely crashes.

27:33.620 --> 27:35.360
It's a phenomenal illustration.

27:35.360 --> 27:40.360
Painting a door shut while you are inside the room versus painting it shut while you are outside the room.

27:40.360 --> 27:47.100
It definitively proves that changing your geometric position and changing the authority of the shared environment do not commute.

27:47.100 --> 27:50.940
They interact in strict, highly directional ways.

27:51.500 --> 27:51.740
Okay.

27:51.940 --> 27:53.620
That's case study one, the file system.

27:54.180 --> 28:00.400
Case study two dives into the world of programming, lexical scope, specifically looking at the Python programming language.

28:00.700 --> 28:00.940
Yes.

28:01.000 --> 28:03.140
This is covered in Proposition 6.3.

28:03.140 --> 28:06.820
When you write code, your variables exist within scopes.

28:07.460 --> 28:09.200
A function has a local scope.

28:09.620 --> 28:11.380
The file itself has a global scope.

28:11.880 --> 28:18.640
If you ask Python what the variable x equals, it has to resolve that name by hunting through the graph of scopes.

28:18.980 --> 28:19.220
Right.

28:19.680 --> 28:24.360
But how it hunts is entirely up to the language's specific resolution policy.

28:24.800 --> 28:27.420
And Python's policy is notoriously weird, right?

28:27.420 --> 28:32.940
The paper talks about how it doesn't just blindly walk up the tree of ancestors looking for the variable.

28:33.700 --> 28:34.600
Oh, far from it.

28:34.920 --> 28:38.300
Python uses a two-pass system that confuses beginners endlessly.

28:39.160 --> 28:45.820
Python actually classifies every single variable in a function body at compile time, before it even runs the code.

28:46.040 --> 28:47.620
Okay, wait, so it looks ahead.

28:47.880 --> 28:48.140
Yes.

28:48.840 --> 28:51.220
The paper gives a really great simple example of this.

28:51.900 --> 28:54.880
Imagine you declare a global variable at the top of your file.

28:55.260 --> 28:56.020
x equals 1.

28:56.020 --> 28:56.580
Got it.

28:57.060 --> 28:57.900
x is 1.

28:58.240 --> 28:59.360
Then you define a function.

29:00.080 --> 29:03.240
Inside the function, on the very first line, you tell it to print x.

29:03.960 --> 29:06.580
But on the very next line, you assign x equals 2.

29:07.320 --> 29:11.040
Logically, as a human reading top to bottom, you'd think it just prints 1.

29:11.520 --> 29:16.960
Because x is 1 globally, and when it hits the print statement, it hasn't even reached the x equals 2 line yet.

29:17.320 --> 29:18.120
That's what you'd think.

29:18.120 --> 29:23.420
But if you run that code, Python actually crashes and throws an unbound local error.

29:23.660 --> 29:24.500
Wait, why?

29:24.880 --> 29:27.440
Because Python scans the entire function body first.

29:27.820 --> 29:35.140
It sees x equals 2 sitting at the bottom, so it classifies x as a local variable for the entire function, including the lines above it.

29:35.260 --> 29:35.660
Oh, wow.

29:35.660 --> 29:36.220
Yeah.

29:36.600 --> 29:45.660
So when it actually executes the print x at the top, it looks for the local x, sees that it hasn't been assigned a value yet in the execution flow, and crashes.

29:46.260 --> 29:54.180
It explicitly refuses to look outside the function for the global x, because the compile time scan already locked x into the local context.

29:54.500 --> 29:55.740
That is wild.

29:55.740 --> 29:59.680
The resolution policy isn't just a simple path up the tree.

29:59.900 --> 30:05.280
It is this complex interaction between a static scan and dynamic execution.

30:05.600 --> 30:06.240
It really is.

30:06.900 --> 30:12.920
And the paper also discusses shadowing in Python, stating that it is mathematically non-injective.

30:13.100 --> 30:17.240
Okay, I need an ELI-5 on non-injective, because that sounds like advanced calculus.

30:17.720 --> 30:19.680
It's actually a fundamental concept of mapping.

30:19.680 --> 30:26.140
A mathematical operation is injective if you can perfectly reverse-engineer the input by looking at the output.

30:26.720 --> 30:30.080
Every unique input gives a completely unique output.

30:30.780 --> 30:31.640
Okay, give me an example.

30:32.080 --> 30:32.240
Sure.

30:32.500 --> 30:39.440
If I have a function that multiplies by 2, and the output is 10, you can reverse-engineer that the input was absolutely 5.

30:39.680 --> 30:40.740
That's injective.

30:41.060 --> 30:42.340
Okay, makes perfect sense.

30:42.340 --> 30:48.740
Now, shadowing is when you declare a variable inside a function with the exact same name as a variable outside the function.

30:49.240 --> 30:52.260
The inner one shadows or hides the outer one.

30:52.500 --> 30:52.700
Right.

30:52.760 --> 30:53.920
You do this all the time in coding.

30:54.060 --> 31:00.500
You have a global count equals 10, but inside a specific loop, you make a local count equals 0.

31:00.700 --> 31:01.040
Exactly.

31:01.640 --> 31:04.060
You're overriding it for that specific block of code.

31:04.380 --> 31:10.240
But mathematically, the operation of extending an environment by shadowing a variable is non-injective.

31:10.840 --> 31:11.840
Let's look at why.

31:11.840 --> 31:12.440
Okay.

31:13.180 --> 31:15.340
See, I have two completely different global environments.

31:15.840 --> 31:17.580
Environment A has x equals 5.

31:18.280 --> 31:20.180
Environment B has x equals 99.

31:21.000 --> 31:26.500
Now, I apply a local extension to both of them that shadows x with a local x equals 2.

31:27.000 --> 31:29.680
What does environment A output if I ask for x?

31:30.200 --> 31:31.380
Well, it outputs 2.

31:31.740 --> 31:32.020
Right.

31:32.160 --> 31:33.480
And what does environment B output?

31:33.900 --> 31:35.440
It also outputs 2.

31:35.720 --> 31:37.200
Ah, I see it.

31:37.200 --> 31:46.820
Looking at the output 2, I have absolutely no mathematical way to reverse engineer whether the original hidden global environment had a 5 or a 99.

31:46.820 --> 31:48.920
The history is completely destroyed.

31:49.240 --> 31:49.600
Exactly.

31:49.600 --> 31:54.080
The observable lookup behavior does not determine the environment that was extended.

31:54.860 --> 31:58.180
Multiple distinct inputs result in the exact same output.

31:58.900 --> 32:00.560
Therefore, it is non-injective.

32:00.560 --> 32:02.980
And that's totally different from how folders work.

32:02.980 --> 32:06.100
This is a massive structural difference from a file system.

32:06.780 --> 32:17.380
In a file system, if you create a file named report.pdf in a subfolder, it doesn't obliterate your ability to access a different report.pdf in the parent folder if you use the full path.

32:17.380 --> 32:19.960
The file system preserves the history.

32:20.540 --> 32:22.060
Lexical scope destroys it.

32:22.620 --> 32:22.820
Okay.

32:23.340 --> 32:25.120
Moving from coding to security.

32:25.940 --> 32:29.320
Case study 3 looks at access control and proposition 7.1.

32:29.540 --> 32:37.860
We touched on this earlier with the sandbox example, but the paper gets very precise about the types of mathematical orders we are dealing with when we talk about authority.

32:38.360 --> 32:38.660
Yes.

32:39.080 --> 32:43.920
When we analyze execution contexts and security, we aren't just dealing with one tree.

32:43.920 --> 32:50.940
There are actually three distinct mathematical orders operating simultaneously, layered right on top of each other.

32:51.340 --> 32:51.360
Okay.

32:51.440 --> 32:51.940
Three orders.

32:52.720 --> 32:54.180
First, there is containment.

32:54.820 --> 32:57.660
This is the geometric nesting we've been talking about.

32:57.760 --> 32:59.880
Folders inside folders, scopes inside scopes.

32:59.960 --> 33:00.140
Right.

33:00.260 --> 33:01.560
The physical feeling hierarchy.

33:02.160 --> 33:04.440
Second, there is the spawn order.

33:05.020 --> 33:07.500
This is the family tree of processes tracking lineage.

33:08.080 --> 33:10.820
Process A launches process B, which launches process C.

33:11.080 --> 33:11.340
Okay.

33:11.400 --> 33:13.020
So a family tree of execution.

33:13.020 --> 33:16.120
And third, there is the delegation order.

33:16.780 --> 33:17.380
Delegation.

33:17.880 --> 33:21.700
Meaning the passing of actual authority or permissions from one entity to another?

33:22.020 --> 33:22.380
Correct.

33:22.740 --> 33:22.980
Okay.

33:23.580 --> 33:27.240
And proposition 7.1 proves a crucial security theorem.

33:28.360 --> 33:31.920
Authority might only decrease strictly along the delegation order.

33:32.500 --> 33:33.220
Just delegation.

33:33.220 --> 33:33.900
Yes.

33:33.900 --> 33:37.240
It is mathematically guaranteed to be monotonic there.

33:37.580 --> 33:43.480
If I only possess the power to read a file, I mathematically cannot delegate the power to write to you.

33:44.040 --> 33:47.380
I can only pass on a subset of my own existing authority.

33:47.920 --> 33:56.160
But, and this is where the security assumptions break down again, authority does not have to decrease along the containment order or the spawn order.

33:56.160 --> 33:56.680
Exactly.

33:56.680 --> 33:56.960
Exactly.

33:57.320 --> 34:00.100
You can actually spawn something far more powerful than yourself.

34:01.160 --> 34:03.040
The paper outlines a really vivid scenario.

34:03.820 --> 34:06.140
Imagine you have a standard user shell.

34:06.400 --> 34:07.240
Let's call it U.

34:07.440 --> 34:07.720
Okay.

34:07.900 --> 34:08.460
Shell U.

34:09.020 --> 34:11.080
That shell launches two things.

34:11.640 --> 34:14.460
A highly restricted sandbox child process.

34:14.600 --> 34:15.400
Let's call it L.

34:15.400 --> 34:19.840
And a program with elevated privileges, like a system admin tool.

34:20.000 --> 34:20.780
Let's call it S.

34:20.780 --> 34:21.240
Okay.

34:21.440 --> 34:24.020
So U is the parent, L and S are the children.

34:24.980 --> 34:28.540
Both L and S are nested physically inside U's directory structure.

34:29.060 --> 34:29.820
That's containment.

34:30.580 --> 34:33.000
Both L and S were launched directly by U.

34:33.460 --> 34:34.400
That's spawn.

34:34.920 --> 34:36.240
But look at their authority.

34:36.980 --> 34:37.540
Right.

34:37.800 --> 34:41.160
The sandbox L has strictly less authority than U.

34:41.160 --> 34:46.280
But the admin tool S has significantly more authority than U.

34:46.760 --> 34:48.320
Even though U is the one who spawned it.

34:48.460 --> 34:49.640
Even though U spawned it.

34:49.880 --> 34:53.540
And even though S is geometrically contained inside U.

34:54.340 --> 35:01.760
The admin tool draws its elevated authority from its own file permissions, or from external system capabilities, not from the user that launched it.

35:02.200 --> 35:04.360
So it bypasses the parent.

35:04.880 --> 35:10.060
Therefore, authority is mathematically completely decoupled from the tree of containment and the tree of spawning.

35:10.060 --> 35:20.240
So for a cybersecurity analyst, this means if they are auditing a system and only looking at a diagram of what folders are inside what folders, the containment tree, they are flying completely blind.

35:20.380 --> 35:25.180
They have to map the invisible delegation network, which might look completely different than the folder structure.

35:26.020 --> 35:29.040
If you assume depth equals security, you will get hacked.

35:29.720 --> 35:30.920
It is a mathematical certainty.

35:31.560 --> 35:32.160
All right.

35:32.200 --> 35:33.120
The final case study.

35:33.380 --> 35:34.100
Interactive worlds.

35:35.020 --> 35:35.720
Video games.

35:36.700 --> 35:38.940
This is propositions 8.1 and 8.2.

35:38.940 --> 35:41.260
I found this part fascinating.

35:41.480 --> 35:44.260
How exactly do video games break the math of context?

35:45.040 --> 35:48.180
They break it beautifully through something called modal returns.

35:48.840 --> 35:49.860
Modal returns.

35:50.500 --> 35:50.720
Yeah.

35:51.000 --> 35:52.800
Let's say you are playing a massive RPG.

35:53.300 --> 35:55.060
You are exploring a deep dungeon.

35:55.780 --> 35:57.460
That is your current spatial context.

35:58.240 --> 36:00.300
Suddenly you press a button to open your inventory.

36:01.340 --> 36:03.340
Opening the menu shifts your context.

36:03.840 --> 36:06.860
You are no longer in the dungeon, mathematically speaking.

36:06.860 --> 36:10.180
You are now navigating the logical context of the menu.

36:10.660 --> 36:11.100
Right.

36:11.500 --> 36:13.800
The game pauses and I'm sorting my potions.

36:14.120 --> 36:14.260
No.

36:14.680 --> 36:16.880
What happens when you press the button to close the menu?

36:17.680 --> 36:18.880
Where do you actually go?

36:19.600 --> 36:20.780
Well, back to the game.

36:21.440 --> 36:21.680
Right.

36:21.700 --> 36:24.240
But you don't go to a fixed hard-coded geometric node.

36:24.900 --> 36:26.040
You return to your origin.

36:26.040 --> 36:32.280
The action, close, dynamically targets whatever context you were standing in before you opened

36:32.280 --> 36:32.660
the menu.

36:33.600 --> 36:34.600
Oh, I see the problem.

36:35.060 --> 36:38.320
If I open the menu in the dungeon, hitting close takes me back to the dungeon.

36:38.720 --> 36:43.040
But if I open the exact same menu while standing in the town shop, hitting close takes me back

36:43.040 --> 36:43.620
to the shop.

36:44.260 --> 36:48.220
Mathematically, this is an absolute nightmare to model on a standard directed graph.

36:48.880 --> 36:52.980
In a normal graph, an action is an arrow pointing from node A to node B.

36:53.120 --> 36:54.480
Always pointing to the same place.

36:54.480 --> 36:55.080
Exactly.

36:55.740 --> 36:59.380
But here, the target of the close action is not a function of the menu itself.

37:00.000 --> 37:02.520
The menu doesn't have a fixed arrow pointing anywhere.

37:02.960 --> 37:06.480
The return target is dynamically pulled from a memory stack.

37:06.900 --> 37:11.620
And Flyxion points out that this makes game graphs theoretically non-finite.

37:12.120 --> 37:13.640
Yes, proposition 8.2.

37:14.100 --> 37:16.920
Imagine menus that can open other menus.

37:17.480 --> 37:18.880
You open the main inventory.

37:19.500 --> 37:21.600
From there, you open a submenu for potions.

37:21.600 --> 37:26.060
From there, you open a detailed lore screen to examine a specific potion.

37:26.680 --> 37:28.080
You're just drilling down deeper and deeper.

37:28.560 --> 37:29.720
You are building a stack.

37:30.080 --> 37:36.840
If the game doesn't place a hard-coded limit on how deep that stack can go, say, letting

37:36.840 --> 37:42.440
you infinitely loop between examining a potion and opening a glossary term, the context component

37:42.440 --> 37:45.040
is mathematically no longer a finite state machine.

37:45.040 --> 37:48.900
It becomes a push-down system capable of infinite depth.

37:48.900 --> 37:54.700
So a simple, everyday video game inventory menu is mathematically vastly more complex than

37:54.700 --> 37:59.500
a standard computer directory structure because it has to remember an infinitely expanding

37:59.500 --> 38:01.140
chain of where you came from.

38:01.280 --> 38:01.720
Precisely.

38:02.460 --> 38:05.640
Now, synthesize all four of these case studies for a moment.

38:06.160 --> 38:10.480
We have file systems with their permanent shared state and strict directional commutation.

38:10.700 --> 38:10.940
Right.

38:10.940 --> 38:16.240
We have lexical scopes with their non-injective shadowing that completely destroys history.

38:17.080 --> 38:20.620
We have access control with its independent invisible delegation order.

38:21.180 --> 38:24.600
And we have video games with their infinite modal return stacks.

38:25.260 --> 38:27.520
You literally cannot treat them as the same thing.

38:27.720 --> 38:28.340
You cannot?

38:29.240 --> 38:32.020
They are entirely different geometric realities.

38:33.000 --> 38:37.940
Calling them all trees is like calling a bicycle, a nuclear submarine, and a Boeing 747

38:37.940 --> 38:42.860
vehicles, and assuming they all steer exactly the same way just because they transport you.

38:43.040 --> 38:43.360
Wow.

38:44.360 --> 38:48.700
Okay, so contexts are vastly, wildly diverse.

38:48.960 --> 38:49.960
They are different shapes.

38:50.140 --> 38:52.260
But here is the next logical hurdle.

38:52.720 --> 38:56.680
Despite being different shapes, they all have to share information up and down their structures.

38:57.280 --> 38:59.580
When a system is running, data has to flow.

38:59.960 --> 39:00.260
Always.

39:00.540 --> 39:04.960
This brings us to section five of the paper, the direction of information flow.

39:04.960 --> 39:11.080
And we finally get to talk about pre-sheaves, which I admit sounds like a very fancy type

39:11.080 --> 39:12.620
of French bread, but I know it's not.

39:12.980 --> 39:14.140
Sadly, no carbs involved.

39:14.520 --> 39:19.940
So a pre-sheave is actually this incredibly dense mathematical tool pulled straight from

39:19.940 --> 39:20.700
category theory.

39:21.140 --> 39:28.100
To explain it simply, it is a way to assign data or local descriptions to different contexts

39:28.100 --> 39:34.180
and rigorously map how that data restricts or changes as you move between those contexts.

39:34.180 --> 39:39.840
It mathematically encodes how descriptions remain consistent when you look at them at different

39:39.840 --> 39:40.380
resolutions.

39:40.980 --> 39:44.140
Okay, so it tracks local data as we zoom in and out.

39:44.340 --> 39:44.880
Exactly.

39:45.760 --> 39:51.600
And Flyxion uses pre-sheaves to prove a massive structural divide between two fundamental types

39:51.600 --> 39:55.680
of systems, regional systems and accumulation systems.

39:55.820 --> 39:57.800
This is Proposition 13.7.

39:58.220 --> 40:00.000
Let's unpack regional systems first.

40:00.020 --> 40:00.460
What are they?

40:00.460 --> 40:02.580
Regional systems are spatial.

40:03.300 --> 40:06.420
Things like file systems or geometric game maps.

40:07.120 --> 40:11.920
In a regional system, the universe is the whole disk or the whole world map.

40:12.140 --> 40:13.040
Okay, the big picture.

40:13.260 --> 40:18.720
As you move deeper into the system, say moving from the root C drive into a specific buried

40:18.720 --> 40:23.540
subfolder, the amount of information you can actively observe actually decreases.

40:23.940 --> 40:24.240
Right.

40:24.240 --> 40:28.300
If I'm searching at the root drive, I am searching millions of files.

40:28.860 --> 40:34.080
But if I move deep into a folder named Receipts 2024, my scope severely narrows.

40:34.400 --> 40:38.100
I am only observing the contents of that specific tiny subtree.

40:38.380 --> 40:38.780
Exactly.

40:39.240 --> 40:41.440
The arrows of information flow point outward.

40:42.120 --> 40:44.360
The larger the region, the richer the observation.

40:44.940 --> 40:47.380
Moving inward is a process of restriction.

40:48.080 --> 40:51.100
Now contrast that with accumulation systems.

40:51.100 --> 40:52.980
Accumulation systems.

40:53.820 --> 40:57.680
Like lexical scope encoding, where we were shadowing variables earlier?

40:57.940 --> 40:58.180
Yes.

40:58.960 --> 41:01.560
Think about writing a deeply nested function in Python.

41:02.260 --> 41:06.240
At the outermost, global level, you can only see global variables.

41:06.980 --> 41:11.700
But as you nest a function inside a function inside a function, the innermost function can

41:11.700 --> 41:17.240
see its own local variables, plus the variables of its parent function, plus the global variables

41:17.240 --> 41:18.320
of the entire file.

41:18.320 --> 41:19.860
Oh, wow.

41:19.980 --> 41:21.160
So it's the exact opposite.

41:21.540 --> 41:26.340
As I move deeper into the structure, the amount of information I observe actually increases.

41:26.580 --> 41:28.040
I am accumulating context.

41:28.580 --> 41:29.100
Exactly.

41:29.680 --> 41:31.680
You see, the entire ancestor chain.

41:32.100 --> 41:35.060
This is what Flyarion calls the orientation duality.

41:35.700 --> 41:38.740
In a regional system, moving inward decreases information.

41:39.320 --> 41:42.860
In an accumulation system, moving inward increases information.

41:43.300 --> 41:45.200
Here's where it gets really interesting for me.

41:45.360 --> 41:46.940
I was trying to visualize this.

41:46.940 --> 41:50.460
It's like comparing a microscope to a telescope, right?

41:50.660 --> 41:51.320
Oh, I like that.

41:51.580 --> 41:53.920
They are essentially the same physical structure, right?

41:54.020 --> 41:56.220
They are both tubes with lenses at either end.

41:56.400 --> 42:00.140
But they flow information in perfectly opposite directions.

42:00.280 --> 42:02.660
A microscope is a regional system.

42:03.500 --> 42:05.320
It zooms in, restricting your view.

42:05.720 --> 42:10.960
It cuts out the rest of the universe to focus on a tiny, restricted subtree on a glass slide.

42:10.960 --> 42:14.880
Moving inward decreases the scope of reality you see.

42:15.600 --> 42:15.820
Yes.

42:16.020 --> 42:17.320
That is spot on.

42:17.980 --> 42:20.140
But a telescope looks deeper into the sky.

42:20.340 --> 42:23.360
And as it looks deeper, it acts as an accumulation system.

42:23.560 --> 42:30.040
It is gathering and accumulating more light from the past, expanding the visible information the further out it looks.

42:30.040 --> 42:33.760
That is an excellent way to conceptualize the orientation duality.

42:33.760 --> 42:41.380
The hardware, the tube and the lenses, or in our case, the nodes and branches of the digital tree, is structurally identical.

42:42.140 --> 42:46.880
But the orientation of the information flow completely changes the reality of what is observed.

42:46.880 --> 42:50.160
So the structure alone isn't enough to define the system.

42:51.100 --> 42:58.020
Flyxion proves mathematically that you can build a file system and a lexical scope on the exact same structural tree.

42:58.400 --> 43:04.220
But because the arrows of information flow point in opposite directions, by proposition 13.7,

43:04.600 --> 43:08.240
no mathematical isomorphism can ever map one to the other.

43:08.760 --> 43:13.780
If the tree has any branching at all, they are fundamentally, irreconcilably different beasts.

43:13.780 --> 43:20.300
Okay, so if the flow of information is so heavily dependent on the geometry and direction, it leads to a huge question.

43:20.740 --> 43:24.540
Can we ever truly understand the whole system just by looking at its local parts?

43:24.860 --> 43:25.860
That is the ultimate question.

43:26.100 --> 43:30.720
If I am only looking through the microscope, can I reliably deduce what the telescope sees?

43:31.120 --> 43:34.860
This brings us to section 6 of the paper, The Local to Global Puzzle.

43:35.100 --> 43:38.480
This section deals with the concept of global reconstruction.

43:38.960 --> 43:41.460
It asks a profound question about reality.

43:41.460 --> 43:50.460
If you gather enough local snapshots of a system, can you seamlessly glue them together to construct the objective global truth?

43:51.220 --> 43:55.800
And to figure this out, the paper brings in growth and deke topologies and the gluing criterion.

43:56.340 --> 43:58.240
Look, you have to ELI-5 this for me.

43:58.380 --> 44:00.400
What on earth is a growth and deke topology?

44:00.700 --> 44:02.220
Don't let the name intimidate you.

44:02.860 --> 44:08.520
In simple terms, a topology is a way of defining what constitutes a cover for a space.

44:08.520 --> 44:12.960
Imagine you have a massive map of the world, but it's torn into a hundred pieces.

44:13.520 --> 44:14.660
Okay, a puzzle, basically.

44:15.280 --> 44:20.260
A growth and deke topology defines the mathematical rules for how those local patches overlap.

44:21.180 --> 44:25.560
The gluing criterion asks, do the overlapping edges match up perfectly?

44:26.100 --> 44:32.120
And if they do match up, does the resulting stitched together map represent a physically possible globe?

44:32.120 --> 44:36.360
Okay, so it's the math of stitching local views into a global reality.

44:37.160 --> 44:42.400
And Flyxion walks us through mathematical failure modes where this gluing process completely falls apart.

44:42.840 --> 44:45.240
Let's look at the first failure mode, obstruction.

44:45.860 --> 44:50.300
This is example 10.4 in the paper, the locally acyclic dependencies.

44:50.840 --> 44:53.260
This is a classic architectural paradox.

44:54.100 --> 44:56.220
Imagine a system with three software modules.

44:56.600 --> 44:58.920
Module 1, Module 2, and Module 3.

44:58.920 --> 45:02.060
We have a strict global rule for this system.

45:02.640 --> 45:05.100
Dependencies must flow in one direction.

45:05.600 --> 45:06.940
No infinite loops are allowed.

45:07.300 --> 45:08.140
Right, no cycles.

45:08.420 --> 45:10.820
So you take a local snapshot of Module 1 and 2.

45:11.520 --> 45:13.780
You see that Module 1 depends on Module 2.

45:14.320 --> 45:15.500
That is a straight line.

45:15.840 --> 45:16.880
It's totally legal.

45:17.120 --> 45:17.320
Right.

45:17.760 --> 45:19.560
Local snapshot A is good.

45:19.640 --> 45:20.200
No loops there.

45:20.560 --> 45:22.780
Then you take a local snapshot of Module 2 and 3.

45:23.500 --> 45:25.340
Module 2 depends on Module 3.

45:26.100 --> 45:28.260
Local snapshot B is also perfectly legal.

45:28.260 --> 45:29.400
Just a straight line.

45:29.920 --> 45:30.300
Still good.

45:30.960 --> 45:33.820
Finally, you take a snapshot of Module 3 and 1.

45:34.520 --> 45:36.360
Module 3 depends on Module 1.

45:37.240 --> 45:39.880
Local snapshot C is also perfectly legal on its own.

45:40.460 --> 45:40.820
Wait a minute.

45:41.420 --> 45:48.420
If 1 depends on 2 and 2 depends on 3, and 3 depends on 1, that's a massive infinite loop.

45:48.800 --> 45:49.160
Exactly.

45:49.520 --> 45:51.320
But here is the terrifying part mathematically.

45:51.320 --> 45:57.880
If you are an observer who only has access to local contexts, you literally cannot see the loop.

45:58.540 --> 46:01.560
Every individual pair of dependencies looks mathematically sound.

46:02.380 --> 46:07.140
The local data is entirely consistent on all the overlaps where you try to glue them together.

46:07.140 --> 46:14.280
But when you synthesize them to form a global reality, they form an impossible, unrealizable cycle.

46:14.280 --> 46:16.880
This is literally an M.C. Escher drawing.

46:17.320 --> 46:19.740
Is this like taking close-up photos of an Escher staircase?

46:20.240 --> 46:24.140
If I take a picture of just two steps, it looks like a normal, functional staircase.

46:24.860 --> 46:27.060
Every individual step obeys gravity.

46:27.340 --> 46:27.540
Yes.

46:27.740 --> 46:28.620
Locally, it's perfect.

46:29.300 --> 46:31.480
Every local snapshot is physically possible.

46:31.840 --> 46:38.160
But when you stitch the photos together and zoom out, the geometry loops back on itself in a way that completely breaks physics.

46:38.320 --> 46:39.860
The building shouldn't exist.

46:39.860 --> 46:42.860
That is the exact visual representation of an obstruction.

46:43.440 --> 46:45.980
The local views are perfectly mathematically consistent.

46:46.680 --> 46:48.940
But the global realization is impossible.

46:49.760 --> 46:58.020
The global invariant, the rule that says no cycles, is a non-local constraint that is completely invisible to every single local observer.

46:58.760 --> 47:00.260
Okay, so that's an obstruction.

47:00.580 --> 47:00.820
Right.

47:00.980 --> 47:04.500
The puzzle pieces fit together perfectly, but the resulting picture is a lie.

47:04.980 --> 47:06.980
What about the other failure mode, ambiguity?

47:07.980 --> 47:09.380
Example 10.5.

47:09.860 --> 47:12.260
Ambiguity is the exact opposite problem.

47:12.780 --> 47:17.600
This is when the global reality has a secret variable that none of the local snapshots can see.

47:18.380 --> 47:20.920
Flyxion calls it the global-only observable.

47:21.240 --> 47:24.260
How does something exist globally but remain totally invisible locally?

47:24.900 --> 47:26.260
Let's build a really simple system.

47:26.840 --> 47:29.620
Imagine two local contexts, A and B.

47:30.160 --> 47:37.200
And imagine a global state that consists of three variables, X, Y, and a secret parity bit, P.

47:37.200 --> 47:37.660
Okay.

47:38.060 --> 47:39.680
X, Y, and secret P.

47:40.120 --> 47:45.940
The rules of visibility in this system dictate that context A can only ever see the variable X.

47:46.660 --> 47:49.780
Context B can only ever see the variable Y.

47:50.500 --> 47:53.220
Neither of them has the mathematical authority to see P.

47:53.220 --> 47:56.820
Okay, so P is literally a ghost to the local context?

47:56.820 --> 47:59.880
Now, imagine two distinct global realities.

48:00.380 --> 48:04.140
Reality 1 is the state where X is 0, Y is 0, P is 0.

48:04.600 --> 48:09.200
Reality 2 is the state where X is 0, Y is 0, P is 1.

48:09.200 --> 48:14.080
These are two entirely different global realities because the parity bit is different.

48:14.900 --> 48:19.380
But if you ask context A what it sees in reality 1, it says X is 0.

48:19.720 --> 48:23.620
If you ask it what it sees in reality 2, it says X is 0.

48:24.040 --> 48:25.480
It cannot tell the difference.

48:26.040 --> 48:28.960
And context B says Y is 0 for both realities as well.

48:29.380 --> 48:29.720
Exactly.

48:30.160 --> 48:35.880
So you have two completely distinct global realities that look exactly mathematically identical

48:35.880 --> 48:38.060
from every single local viewpoint.

48:38.060 --> 48:43.880
The local data stitches together perfectly, but it fails to uniquely identify the true global state.

48:44.260 --> 48:45.260
So what is the takeaway here?

48:45.360 --> 48:46.420
What does this mean for us?

48:46.600 --> 48:52.660
This raises a massive philosophical and mathematical question regarding contextuality.

48:53.460 --> 49:01.020
We mathematically cannot always trust local snapshots to glue together to a single coherent global reality.

49:01.300 --> 49:06.600
The absolute truth of the system might exist purely in the unobservable global layer.

49:06.600 --> 49:09.520
That is deeply unsettling.

49:10.240 --> 49:14.760
If local systems are this messy, this prone to Escher paradoxes and hidden variables,

49:15.100 --> 49:17.980
what happens when we try to translate between them?

49:18.560 --> 49:20.440
I mean, computer scientists do this every day.

49:20.700 --> 49:21.380
They absolutely do.

49:21.500 --> 49:26.400
They translate an app from iOS to Android or from Python to C++.

49:26.400 --> 49:30.960
And they assure everyone that the translated system is mathematically equivalent.

49:31.960 --> 49:35.060
Is this equivalence just a comforting lie?

49:35.560 --> 49:38.200
That brings us to Section 7, Lost in Translation.

49:38.200 --> 49:41.600
It's not necessarily a lie, but it is highly conditional.

49:42.260 --> 49:48.980
Flyxion spends this section rigorously defining what translation actually means in a geometric framework.

49:49.540 --> 49:55.660
When you move a complex system into a new language or a new architecture, what survives the journey?

49:56.020 --> 49:57.140
And what dies?

49:57.140 --> 50:03.680
Well, first off, the paper points out that finding a common abstraction between two systems is essentially a parlor trick.

50:03.940 --> 50:05.420
It's too easy to be meaningful.

50:05.800 --> 50:05.920
Right.

50:06.620 --> 50:11.860
Mathematically, you can always map any two systems to what is called a trivial terminal abstraction.

50:12.380 --> 50:18.240
This is a theoretical mathematical universe with exactly one state and one context,

50:18.740 --> 50:21.720
where absolutely everything is visible and absolutely nothing is forbidden.

50:21.720 --> 50:22.320
Okay.

50:22.720 --> 50:24.460
So it's a completely open sandbox.

50:24.840 --> 50:26.860
It is a completely useless mapping.

50:27.360 --> 50:28.220
But it proves a point.

50:28.700 --> 50:33.560
Just because you can find a common abstraction doesn't mean the systems are actually equivalent in practice.

50:34.000 --> 50:39.000
To have a meaningful translation, we have to look very closely at what specific structures are preserved.

50:39.800 --> 50:43.680
So in a good functional translation, what is actually preserved?

50:44.000 --> 50:47.300
Generally, observational indistinguishability is preserved.

50:47.300 --> 50:53.080
If two states look identical to a user in the source system, they should look identical in the target system.

50:53.580 --> 50:54.060
That makes sense.

50:54.760 --> 50:57.260
Contextual reachability is also usually preserved.

50:58.300 --> 51:04.180
If you can navigate from context A to context B in the original app, you should be able to do it in the translated app.

51:04.600 --> 51:06.060
The positive actions survive.

51:06.600 --> 51:07.620
But what is lost?

51:08.200 --> 51:15.180
What is lost is the multiplicity of microstates, the fine-grained timing dynamics, and most critically, prohibitions.

51:16.080 --> 51:16.640
Prohibitions.

51:16.760 --> 51:19.880
This is Proposition 18.2, the safety transfer laws.

51:20.240 --> 51:24.620
This is perhaps the most practical, terrifying warning in the entire paper for software engineers.

51:25.420 --> 51:29.380
Let's say you have a highly secure legacy system, System A.

51:30.380 --> 51:32.100
It has strict geometric rules.

51:32.440 --> 51:34.380
It explicitly forbids certain actions.

51:34.680 --> 51:37.940
For instance, an unprivileged user cannot write to the system core.

51:38.160 --> 51:38.360
Right.

51:38.360 --> 51:44.820
Now, you translate system A into a coarser, more modern abstraction, System B, to run on the web.

51:45.180 --> 51:45.440
Okay.

51:45.900 --> 51:49.480
I port my super-secure banking app to a web portal.

51:50.080 --> 51:54.960
Flyxion proves mathematically that translations inherently only preserve permitted behaviors.

51:55.420 --> 51:55.580
Why?

51:56.240 --> 52:00.420
Because a translation maps positive structures, nodes, and edges in a graph.

52:00.940 --> 52:07.020
If system A has an edge that allows a user to read a file, the compiler translates that edge into system B.

52:07.020 --> 52:07.420
Okay.

52:08.100 --> 52:13.900
But, prohibitions, the rule that user A cannot open file C, aren't objects.

52:14.400 --> 52:16.200
They are the absence of an edge.

52:16.680 --> 52:17.560
They are negative space.

52:18.520 --> 52:20.580
Translation tools look for objects to map.

52:21.160 --> 52:26.160
And you mathematically cannot map a boundary if the new system's geometry is a fundamentally different shape.

52:26.720 --> 52:28.480
So what does this all mean?

52:28.480 --> 52:35.680
If you translate a highly secure system into a core substraction, you might be able to mathematically prove the app works.

52:36.260 --> 52:40.280
The permitted features, logging in, transferring money, they all function perfectly.

52:40.540 --> 52:40.760
Yes.

52:40.920 --> 52:41.880
The functionality works.

52:42.440 --> 52:49.320
But, because the negative space didn't map, you accidentally stripped away the exact structural rules that kept the hackers out.

52:49.700 --> 52:50.160
Exactly.

52:50.160 --> 52:54.040
The math of translation completely discards the negative space.

52:54.040 --> 52:59.680
It's like translating a nuanced 500-page legal contract into a one-page summary for a child.

53:00.160 --> 53:01.700
The summary might accurately say,

53:02.160 --> 53:04.160
Bob agrees to build the house.

53:04.760 --> 53:07.080
The permitted action is perfectly preserved.

53:07.240 --> 53:07.360
Right.

53:07.360 --> 53:11.280
But the summary completely drops the 50 pages of clauses that say,

53:11.740 --> 53:16.080
Bob is strictly prohibited from using cheap, flammable materials and cannot work on Sundays.

53:16.720 --> 53:20.300
You preserve the action, but you completely lost the safety boundary.

53:20.560 --> 53:21.820
That is a perfect analogy.

53:22.500 --> 53:24.580
The coarse image over-approximates behavior.

53:25.240 --> 53:32.300
You can only use a translated model to prove a system is safe if the original system's safety didn't rely on what it forbade.

53:32.860 --> 53:36.140
But cybersecurity is almost entirely about what is forbidden.

53:36.460 --> 53:36.860
Exactly.

53:37.360 --> 53:45.460
The math tells us that relying on a translated abstraction for security proofs is incredibly dangerous because the negative space just doesn't transfer.

53:45.740 --> 53:46.000
Wow.

53:46.680 --> 53:48.460
We are covering so much ground here.

53:48.560 --> 53:49.440
Let's bring it all home.

53:50.000 --> 53:50.920
Section 8.

53:51.300 --> 53:52.680
The limits of the tree.

53:53.040 --> 53:55.720
We are going to look at unfolding and descent.

53:56.160 --> 53:58.280
To close the loop on this deep dive,

53:58.700 --> 54:01.880
Phlyoxian takes this classic tree structure we all rely on,

54:02.120 --> 54:03.780
the one we started the hour defending,

54:03.780 --> 54:07.720
and pushes it until the math literally snaps in half.

54:08.040 --> 54:08.240
Yes.

54:08.420 --> 54:13.160
The final section explores the ultimate mathematical limits of hierarchical representation.

54:14.160 --> 54:18.200
Why do computer scientists try to force everything into a tree in the first place?

54:18.420 --> 54:19.320
Because it's easier?

54:19.740 --> 54:20.580
Historically, yes.

54:21.080 --> 54:25.260
Early hardware and file systems needed simple, predictable memory allocation.

54:25.260 --> 54:29.400
Trees are computationally cheap, graphs are expensive and messy.

54:29.680 --> 54:32.700
But as we've seen, reality is a graph.

54:33.000 --> 54:33.180
Right.

54:33.720 --> 54:38.460
And we already discussed how things like Python's multiple inheritance completely break the tree.

54:38.460 --> 54:43.700
If class D inherits from class B and class C, which both inherit from class A,

54:44.180 --> 54:45.480
that's a diamond shape, not a tree.

54:45.720 --> 54:46.100
Exactly.

54:46.860 --> 54:50.440
And because the tree fails, Python has to use an external algorithm,

54:50.800 --> 54:52.360
the C3 linearization,

54:52.660 --> 54:55.900
to artificially flatten that diamond into a straight line,

54:56.300 --> 54:58.220
just so it knows which method to execute.

54:58.480 --> 54:59.960
So the hierarchy needs help.

55:00.220 --> 55:00.520
Yes.

55:00.700 --> 55:03.820
The hierarchy itself underdetermines reality.

55:04.280 --> 55:06.580
The tree isn't smart enough to resolve the conflict,

55:06.580 --> 55:11.480
so Python has to stable X for math on top of it to decide which parent wins.

55:12.060 --> 55:13.640
The tree needs external life support.

55:14.180 --> 55:16.620
But the most vivid example in the paper of the tree failing

55:16.620 --> 55:20.540
is when we try to force a complex network to actually become a tree

55:20.540 --> 55:23.140
through a mathematical process called unfolding.

55:23.320 --> 55:25.260
This is example 19.4.

55:25.720 --> 55:28.980
Walk me through how unfolding works and how it completely breaks.

55:29.300 --> 55:31.120
Let's go back to video games.

55:31.280 --> 55:32.320
Imagine a game map.

55:32.940 --> 55:34.520
We have a starting hub room.

55:34.520 --> 55:39.220
From the hub, you can take a left path to room A or a right path to room B.

55:40.000 --> 55:44.700
And both room A and room B have a door that leads to the exact same boss room.

55:45.180 --> 55:46.420
Okay, so it's a diamond shape again.

55:46.720 --> 55:48.160
Hub connects to A and B.

55:48.560 --> 55:50.080
A and B converge on the boss room.

55:50.500 --> 55:53.900
Now, in the boss room, there is a physical switch on the wall.

55:53.900 --> 55:57.240
If you flip it, a global light turns on.

55:57.820 --> 56:00.020
The mathematical bit changes from zero to one.

56:00.420 --> 56:00.560
Got it.

56:00.760 --> 56:01.120
I'm with you.

56:01.440 --> 56:05.180
Now, a compiler or a computer scientist comes along and says,

56:05.760 --> 56:06.680
I hate graphs.

56:07.140 --> 56:08.360
They are too hard to process.

56:08.580 --> 56:12.140
I want to represent this game as a pure mathematical tree.

56:12.800 --> 56:13.080
Oh, no.

56:13.740 --> 56:16.780
To do that, they have to perform an unfolding.

56:17.660 --> 56:19.700
They trace every possible path from the root.

56:20.560 --> 56:23.800
Path one is hub to room A to boss room.

56:24.120 --> 56:26.240
Okay, that's the left branch of our new tree.

56:26.780 --> 56:29.900
And path two is hub to room B to boss room.

56:30.100 --> 56:31.200
That is the right branch.

56:31.900 --> 56:33.920
But remember the strict rule of a tree.

56:34.820 --> 56:36.680
Branches can never, ever merge.

56:37.280 --> 56:42.860
So, to make this a tree, the unfolding process has to literally duplicate the boss room.

56:43.660 --> 56:46.260
There is now one boss room at the end of the left branch,

56:46.820 --> 56:50.680
and a completely separate, independent boss room at the end of the right branch.

56:50.680 --> 56:55.440
But wait, in the reality of the game, it's supposed to be the exact same room.

56:55.940 --> 56:57.140
In the original graph, it is.

56:57.400 --> 57:02.200
But in the unfolded tree representation, they are two mathematically distinct nodes.

57:02.900 --> 57:03.820
And here's the paradox.

57:04.560 --> 57:06.440
Say you play the game in the tree version.

57:06.820 --> 57:10.060
You go left through room A into the left boss room.

57:10.600 --> 57:11.440
You flip the switch.

57:12.120 --> 57:12.820
The light turns on.

57:13.220 --> 57:14.040
The bit is one.

57:14.400 --> 57:15.320
Okay, I flipped it.

57:15.620 --> 57:17.200
Then you walk all the way back to the hub,

57:17.840 --> 57:18.800
take the right path,

57:19.500 --> 57:20.300
go through room B,

57:20.860 --> 57:22.200
and enter the right boss room.

57:22.780 --> 57:23.860
What state is the switch in?

57:25.480 --> 57:25.920
It's...

57:25.920 --> 57:27.480
Wait, it's off.

57:27.680 --> 57:29.900
Because it's a completely independent copy of the room,

57:30.380 --> 57:32.040
I toggled the switch in the left universe,

57:32.180 --> 57:33.960
but the right universe doesn't know about it.

57:34.240 --> 57:35.540
The state didn't share.

57:36.020 --> 57:36.420
Exactly.

57:37.240 --> 57:42.580
Forcing the graph into a tree created two completely separate isolated realities of that room.

57:42.720 --> 57:45.600
The sharing of the context is completely lost.

57:46.140 --> 57:48.520
The tree destroyed the truth of the system.

57:48.520 --> 57:51.040
So how do computers actually fix this?

57:51.120 --> 57:52.880
Because obviously games work.

57:52.980 --> 57:56.300
You don't have parallel boss rooms in most games.

57:56.300 --> 58:00.900
To fix this, you have to introduce a massive mathematical patch called the descent condition.

58:01.400 --> 58:03.520
This is proposition 19.3.

58:03.520 --> 58:05.340
What exactly is descent?

58:05.340 --> 58:05.440
What exactly is descent?

58:06.500 --> 58:09.440
Descent is essentially heavy mathematical duct tape.

58:09.980 --> 58:14.240
Use it to force those two separate tree branches to act as one shared reality again.

58:14.240 --> 58:18.580
It is an external rule that constantly monitors the tree and says,

58:18.580 --> 58:25.160
Hey, if the bit changes in the left boss room, instantly teleport that change to the right boss room.

58:25.520 --> 58:28.380
Because structurally they are supposed to be the exact same place.

58:28.380 --> 58:35.720
So forcing a network into a tree structure is like taking a 3D globe and forcing it onto a flat 2D map.

58:36.220 --> 58:41.520
To make it flat, you have to tear the continents apart or stretch the oceans massively.

58:41.520 --> 58:43.820
You completely ruin the geometry.

58:44.640 --> 58:50.340
And descent is the mathematical tape you use to remind the map that Russia and Alaska are actually touching.

58:50.660 --> 58:53.720
Even though you drew them on completely opposite sides of the paper.

58:54.040 --> 58:56.380
That is a beautiful way to visualize it.

58:57.080 --> 59:02.640
A tree representation is mathematically useful for simple computation, but it is structurally incomplete.

59:03.380 --> 59:05.700
It loses the identity of repeated contexts.

59:05.700 --> 59:14.760
It forces you to staple descent conditions, guards, and linearizations all over it just to make it simulate the actual complex graph it's trying to represent.

59:15.160 --> 59:19.360
Okay, we have covered an incredible amount of mathematical and conceptual ground today.

59:19.920 --> 59:21.480
Let's synthesize this journey.

59:22.180 --> 59:31.100
What Flyxion has shown us is that context, the digital spaces we interact with every day, is not just an inert bucket that you throw files or code into.

59:31.420 --> 59:34.960
It is a highly active, bizarre geometric structure.

59:34.960 --> 59:39.840
It mathematically dictates what you know, what you can see, and what you are allowed to do.

59:40.060 --> 59:40.460
Exactly.

59:41.200 --> 59:49.240
And the rules governing those structures, whether it's a Unix shell, a Python script, or a video game inventory, are wildly, fundamentally different.

59:49.600 --> 59:53.800
We really have to stop treating digital architecture as a single, uniform hierarchy.

59:54.540 --> 59:56.980
The tree is a comforting lie that we tell ourselves.

59:56.980 --> 01:00:07.980
The reality is a multidimensional geometry of scope, filled with obstructions, non-injective shadows, and directional information flows that can hide infinite loops and secret variables.

01:00:08.240 --> 01:00:11.300
It really changes how you look at a simple folder on your desktop.

01:00:11.300 --> 01:00:20.760
But to leave you, the listener, with one final provocative thought, at the very end of the paper, Flyxion introduces Proposition 16.3.

01:00:21.480 --> 01:00:23.560
Contexts migrate into data.

01:00:24.220 --> 01:00:28.640
They look at advanced compiler techniques, specifically something called closure conversion.

01:00:28.640 --> 01:00:30.260
Yes, closure conversion.

01:00:30.680 --> 01:00:32.760
This is where the math gets truly existential.

01:00:33.500 --> 01:00:46.100
In these compiler techniques, the entire nested geometry of the source code, all those carefully constructed scopes, the trees, the graphs, the inner and outer boundaries, is completely stripped away during compilation.

01:00:46.780 --> 01:00:47.800
Stripped away completely?

01:00:47.800 --> 01:00:48.400
Yes.

01:00:48.780 --> 01:00:56.700
The compiler flattens all of that beautiful, complex geometry into a single, flat data argument and ships it off to the machine to execute.

01:00:57.220 --> 01:00:58.900
Which is a profound realization.

01:00:59.360 --> 01:01:04.300
The computer's processor ultimately doesn't care about the tree or the graph or the nesting dolls.

01:01:04.500 --> 01:01:07.560
It just processes a flat list of instructions.

01:01:07.960 --> 01:01:08.380
Exactly.

01:01:09.040 --> 01:01:11.980
The geometry is entirely erased at the lowest level.

01:01:11.980 --> 01:01:24.940
So, if the very geometry of your context can be mathematically flattened out of existence and packed into a flat variable, does context even objectively exist inside the machine?

01:01:24.940 --> 01:01:36.760
Or, is this entire geometry of scope we've spent the hour talking about, the folders, the boundaries, the inside and outside, the vaults within vaults, just a comfortable, multidimensional illusion?

01:01:37.500 --> 01:01:45.800
Is it just a digital projection generated entirely for the benefit of the human mind, to keep us from losing our sanity in a sea of flat, contextless data?

01:01:46.340 --> 01:01:47.900
I will let you mull that over.

01:01:48.920 --> 01:01:52.280
A fascinating, slightly terrifying question to end on.

01:01:53.200 --> 01:01:54.860
Thanks for diving deep with us today.

01:01:54.940 --> 01:01:57.020
We'll see you next time on the Edge of the Elysian.

